<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parsis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parsi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parsis are a Zoroastrian community in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> after the Arab conquest. They preserved the religious tradition that had been the state faith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>. Though small in number, they have had a significant cultural and economic influence in India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parsis.jpg" alt="Parsis" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_-_Leaf_from_Bound_Collection_of_20_Miniatures_Depicting_Village_Life_-_Walters_35176H.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Parsis are an ethnoreligious community of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. According to the Qissa-i Sanjan, a Parsi epic of the 16th century and the only account of their early years, they descend from Zoroastrian Persians who fled persecution after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muslim-conquest-of-persia">Arab conquest of Iran</a>. It was composed at least six centuries after their tentative date of arrival and places the first group in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>. The earliest migrants settled among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> after Jadi Rana, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> king of Sanjan, gave them refuge. A Parsi priest answered his fear that the kingdom was full by adding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> to a vessel of milk, to show that they would blend in and enrich the local culture.</p><p>The Parsis are the older of the subcontinent's two Zoroastrian communities. The other, the Irani community, descends from migrants of Qajar Iran. Their mother tongue is Gujarati or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, and the word Parsi means Persian. The migration from the Sasanian lands continued between the 7th and 10th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Qissa says little about the centuries after the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanjan">Sanjan</a> beyond the establishment of the Fire of Victory, an Atash Bahram, at Sanjan and its later move to Navsari. Until the 17th century most Parsis lived in farming communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. After the commercial treaty between the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James I of England</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> gained the right to build factories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surat">Surat</a>, and many Parsis moved to the English settlements to take the new jobs. When the company transferred its headquarters from Surat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in 1687, the Parsis followed, and they soon held posts of trust in government and public works.</p><p>In the 1860s and 1870s the linguist Martin Haug interpreted Zoroastrian scripture in Christian terms, at a time when English and American missionaries were criticising the Parsis for polytheism. The community, which had no theologians of its own, largely accepted his reading.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Who counts as a Parsi is contested. It is generally accepted that a Parsi is descended from the original Persian refugees and has been admitted to the faith through the navjote ceremony, which is held when a child can recite the required prayers, ideally before puberty. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> counted 57,264 Parsis, and the community lives mostly in cities, with its highest density in Greater Mumbai.</p><p>Traditionally the dead are taken to a Tower of Silence, because earth, fire and water are sacred and are not to be defiled by the dead. The Parsis became highly influential in trade, industry, culture and philanthropy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamsetji-tata">Jamsetji Tata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a> are among their best-known members.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parsis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British India was a predominantly rural society of peasant cultivators, organised by caste, religion and region, with Hindus forming the majority and Muslims the largest minority, alongside Sikhs, Christians, Jains, <b>Parsis</b> and tribal peoples.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Zoroastrianism, though reduced to a minority faith in Iran after the conquest, survived in the <b>Parsi</b> communities of western India, descendants of Zoroastrian migrants from Iran, and continues as a living tradition whose cultural influence far exceeds its small numbers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Parsis</b> of India later produced texts in Gujarati and Persian, among them the Story of Sanjan, the traditional account of their migration from Iran.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Dadabhai Naoroji, a <b>Parsi</b> from Bombay, argued that British rule drained India’s wealth through the salaries, pensions, interest and profits sent to Britain, at a time when the famine of 1876–1878 killed millions.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Some Zoroastrians left Iran: by the Story of Sanjan a group landed at Sanjan in Gujarat, at a date between the 8th and 10th centuries that remains uncertain, and their descendants became the <b>Parsis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>Long-distance trade lay largely with communities such as the Banias, Marwaris and Khatris, the Muslim Bohras, the Armenians and the <b>Parsis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li></ul></section>
